01:07:26,110 --> 01:07:27,050
Present versus past.

1174
01:07:27,210 --> 01:07:27,570
Got it.

1175
01:07:28,010 --> 01:07:35,300
Regarding children, basic clear statements include, I have a seven-year-old son, I'm a single parent, or I'm a mother of twins.

1176
01:07:36,060 --> 01:07:37,420
These are concise and informative.

1177
01:07:37,760 --> 01:07:47,650
This is where we can really elevate the conversation when talking about family and something that makes your introduction far more personal, relatable, and inviting.

1178
01:07:48,310 --> 01:08:01,410
Our guidance strongly suggests that adding a little bit of commentary makes your statement more interesting and crucially provides hints and clues for the other person to continue the conversation in a really positive way.

1179
01:08:02,130 --> 01:08:03,150
Opening the door for them.

1180
01:08:03,170 --> 01:08:03,750
Exactly.

1181
01:08:03,830 --> 01:08:09,620
It's about opening doors for genuine dialogue, not closing them with a mere factual statement.

1182
01:08:09,600 --> 01:08:13,620
For example, instead of just saying I'm a mother of twins, consider elaborating slightly.

1183
01:08:13,640 --> 01:08:14,540
I'm a single mom.

1184
01:08:14,600 --> 01:08:15,390
I've got twin girls.

1185
01:08:15,450 --> 01:08:17,770
They're five years old and they just started school this year.

1186
01:08:17,850 --> 01:08:19,140
So it's been pretty exciting for us.

1187
01:08:19,310 --> 01:08:21,730
See, that gives me something to ask about school.

1188
01:08:21,830 --> 01:08:22,390
How exciting.

1189
01:08:22,430 --> 01:08:23,210
How are they finding it?

1190
01:08:23,520 --> 01:08:23,720
Right.

1191
01:08:23,760 --> 01:08:29,070
This little anecdote transforms a simple statement into a mini story, a glimpse into your life.

1192
01:08:29,650 --> 01:08:36,260
This kind of detail genuinely invites natural follow up questions from the listener, such as, oh, what school are they going to?

1193
01:08:36,280 --> 01:08:40,050
Or, wow, now that they're at school, you must feel like you've got.

1194
01:08:40,030 --> 01:08:42,150
So much more time to yourself again.

1195
01:08:42,190 --> 01:08:48,190
These questions help bridge the gap, building genuine connection in any setting, be it social or professional.

1196
01:08:48,540 --> 01:08:50,760
They show you've given them something to engage with.

1197
01:08:50,900 --> 01:08:52,600
Makes the conversation flow much better.

1198
01:08:52,820 --> 01:08:56,520
When discussing siblings, you can, of course, say, I've got three sisters.

1199
01:08:57,120 --> 01:09:04,690
But our research points out that explicitly listing, I've got two brothers and three sisters, can be a bit of a mouthful and slightly clunky.

1200
01:09:04,960 --> 01:09:05,580
Yeah, a bit listy.

1201
01:09:05,680 --> 01:09:09,980
So a more concise and elegant option is to use the general term siblings.

1202
01:09:10,580 --> 01:09:12,290
For example, I have three siblings.

1203
01:09:13,010 --> 01:09:16,750
It's important to note, and this is a common point of confusion,

1204
01:09:16,930 --> 01:09:18,820
When you say that, it doesn't include you.

1205
01:09:18,860 --> 01:09:19,520
Right.

1206
01:09:19,680 --> 01:09:20,400
Three other people.

1207
01:09:20,680 --> 01:09:21,020
Correct.

1208
01:09:21,760 --> 01:09:27,370
If you want to include yourself in the total count, you would phrase it as, I am one of four siblings.

1209
01:09:28,310 --> 01:09:34,260
And if you have no brothers or sisters, the simple and clear statement is, I'm an only child.

1210
01:09:35,150 --> 01:09:35,410
Okay.

1211
01:09:35,530 --> 01:09:36,750
Clear distinctions there.

1212
01:09:37,050 --> 01:09:47,210
Finally, for talking about your broader network of relatives like aunties, uncles, grandparents, cousins, the term extended family is a really, really great expression to use.

1213
01:09:47,290 --> 01:09:48,110
Very useful term.

1214
01:09:48,550 --> 01:09:55,700
It's a concise and efficient way to include all those relatives without having to list everyone out individually, which would quickly bore your listener.

1215
01:09:56,240 --> 01:10:00,290
You could say, I live in Perth, but most of my extended family lives in Melbourne.

1216
01:10:00,710 --> 01:10:10,120
This provides a broader personal context efficiently, which can be particularly useful when you need to quickly establish personal background without getting bogged down in an exhaustive list.

1217
01:10:10,140 --> 01:10:12,940
It's about sharing just enough to create a connection.

1218
01:10:13,120 --> 01:10:14,440
Efficient and informative.

1219
01:10:14,460 --> 01:10:20,850
Now, for what many of us consider the fun stuff, things that truly reveal your personality, your passions and what makes you, well, you.

1220
01:10:20,970 --> 01:10:21,650
The good stuff.

1221
01:10:21,930 --> 01:10:23,270
The good stuff.

1222
01:10:23,250 --> 01:10:29,900
Beyond the essential facts, you absolutely want to consider sharing what interests or hobbies you do outside of work.

1223
01:10:30,700 --> 01:10:43,660
This is often where you can genuinely connect with people on a deeper, more personal level, which can be incredibly beneficial for building rapport and memorable interactions in any situation, including crucial informal business networking events.

1224
01:10:43,640 --> 01:10:45,260
People connect over shared interests.

1225
01:10:45,420 --> 01:10:45,820
Totally.

1226
01:10:46,120 --> 01:10:50,850
People often remember how you made them feel, and finding common interests is a fantastic way to do that.

1227
01:10:51,490 --> 01:10:57,320
Our analysis gives us some excellent phrasing options for talking about your hobbies, allowing you to sound natural and confident.

1228
01:10:57,720 --> 01:11:04,510
You can effectively use I enjoy plus noungerend, such as I enjoy baking, or I enjoy hiking in the mountains.

1229
01:11:04,770 --> 01:11:06,810
Enjoy plus the eng form or a noun.

1230
01:11:06,880 --> 01:11:07,720
Exactly.

1231
01:11:07,740 --> 01:11:11,780
This is a solid, clear, and grammatically correct way to express your likes.

1232
01:11:12,400 --> 01:11:18,350
And here's a crucial grammar rule to remember, which our research explicitly states and is a common pitfall.

1233
01:11:19,510 --> 01:11:21,270
Don't say, I enjoy to bake.

1234
01:11:21,890 --> 01:11:26,020
The verb enjoy is always followed by a noun, and this is a gerund.

1235
01:11:26,840 --> 01:11:34,270
Getting the small but significant detail right is a hallmark of confident and fluent English communication, showing attention to detail.

1236
01:11:34,610 --> 01:11:36,730
Good reminder, enjoy plus gerund.

1237
01:11:36,780 --> 01:11:40,120
With I like love, you have a bit more flexibility, which is great.

1238
01:11:40,140 --> 01:11:42,350
You can say I like love plus noun gerund.

1239
01:11:42,370 --> 01:11:47,180
For example, I love playing football, or I like reading novels, or I like love to verb infinitive.

1240
01:11:47,200 --> 01:11:50,510
For example, I love to play football, or I like to read novels.

1241
01:11:50,520 --> 01:11:52,590
Either the gerund or the infinitive works there.

1242
01:11:52,610 --> 01:11:52,730
Yep.

1243
01:11:52,890 --> 01:11:57,560
The meaning stays exactly the same, so you can choose whichever sounds more natural and comfortable for you in the moment.

1244
01:11:57,820 --> 01:11:58,700
Nice to have options.

1245
01:11:58,800 --> 01:12:05,970
And a really natural, modern, and popular way of talking about things you like is to say, I'm really into something.

1246
01:12:06,150 --> 01:12:12,300
For instance, I'm really into jazz music, or I'm really into vintage cars, or I'm really into learning new languages.

1247
01:12:12,980 --> 01:12:19,740
This expression is casual, engaging, and instantly conveys enthusiasm, making you sound approachable and passionate.

1248
01:12:20,250 --> 01:12:22,750
into shows really enthusiasm.

1249
01:12:23,430 --> 01:12:24,010
I like that one.

1250
01:12:24,690 --> 01:12:31,630
If we connect this to the bigger picture, having a couple of interesting things to include about yourself is so useful in lots of different contexts.

1251
01:12:31,850 --> 01:12:32,750
Like icebreakers.

1252
01:12:32,990 --> 01:12:33,450
Exactly.

1253
01:12:33,890 --> 01:12:39,960
Think about those common icebreaker scenarios that pop up in new classes, team meetings, or even during job interviews.

1254
01:12:39,940 --> 01:12:47,270
where the interviewer consciously or subconsciously tries to gauge your personality and cultural fit beyond your raw qualifications.

1255
01:12:47,790 --> 01:12:54,040
Teachers and facilitators often kick off with prompts asking for your name, where you're from, and something interesting about yourself.

1256
01:12:54,060 --> 01:12:55,420
Oh yeah, that classic combo.

1257
01:12:55,760 --> 01:13:04,820
Being prepared with a unique yet appropriate passion point allows you to present a distinct facet of your personality and make a memorable impact.

1258
01:13:05,510 --> 01:13:10,230
Our research offers a fantastic opener for this, a kind of conversational hook.

1259
01:13:10,380 --> 01:13:13,710
One thing you may not know about me is... Ooh, intriguing.

1260
01:13:13,730 --> 01:13:19,010
It immediately hooks the listener, signals that something intriguing and personal was coming, and makes them lean in.

1261
01:13:19,070 --> 01:13:27,740
For example, one thing you may not know about me is I love to have elaborate dinner parties, and I'll spend whole days preparing and organizing themed events for my friends.

1262
01:13:28,160 --> 01:13:29,030
Wow, okay.

1263
01:13:29,050 --> 01:13:30,150
That tells you a lot.

1264
01:13:30,170 --> 01:13:30,530
Doesn't it?

1265
01:13:30,810 --> 01:13:31,570
It's not just a fact.

1266
01:13:32,010 --> 01:13:37,120
It's a vibrant window into your passions, creativity, and perhaps even your organizational skills.

1267
01:13:37,100 --> 01:13:43,490
Or one thing you may not know about me is I'm training for a marathon and just completed my first half marathon last month.

1268
01:13:43,710 --> 01:13:46,380
Also impressive and a great conversation starter.

1269
01:13:46,440 --> 01:13:47,170
Exactly.

1270
01:13:47,590 --> 01:13:52,030
The analysis here is that such a statement serves as an excellent, interesting conversation starter.

1271
01:13:52,590 --> 01:13:54,180
It creates an immediate talking point.

1272
01:13:54,760 --> 01:14:03,190
It allows you to share a special talent or an interesting hobby or a unique achievement that might not naturally come up in a typical, more formal introduction.

1273
01:14:03,850 --> 01:14:14,530
In a business English context, these carefully chosen personal details can make you far more memorable, approachable, and relatable, fostering stronger professional connections and networking opportunities.

1274
01:14:14,510 --> 01:14:21,060
It helps you stand out in a positive, authentic way, creating an impression that goes beyond just your resume.

1275
01:14:21,420 --> 01:14:22,560
Makes you more three-dimensional.

1276
01:14:22,920 --> 01:14:27,130
Moving firmly into the professional realm now, of course, you will frequently want to talk about your job.

1277
01:14:27,830 --> 01:14:38,570
This is particularly relevant and critical in business English learning, where clearly articulating your role, responsibilities, and the company you work for is fundamental to establishing your professional identity.

1278
01:14:38,870 --> 01:14:40,750
Core information in many contexts.

1279
01:14:40,900 --> 01:14:41,480
Absolutely.

1280
01:14:41,660 --> 01:14:46,810
Usually when introducing yourself in this context, you're primarily sharing what you do and maybe who you work for.

1281
01:14:47,290 --> 01:14:56,090
But, and this is a point our research highlights as absolutely crucial, accuracy is key here, especially when it comes to those often tricky prepositions and articles.

1282
01:14:56,430 --> 01:14:58,500
Ah, yes, the little words that matter so much.

1283
01:14:58,520 --> 01:14:59,120
They really do.

1284
01:14:59,160 --> 01:15:10,270
These small grammatical elements can be really challenging to get right in this section, so paying close, meticulous attention is paramount to sounding polished and professional.

1285
01:15:10,250 --> 01:15:13,830
Let's break down the precise phrasing for discussing your job position.

1286
01:15:13,890 --> 01:15:17,400
The standard way is, I am a Hessian plus job position.

1287
01:15:17,650 --> 01:15:21,940
For instance, I'm a graphic designer, I'm an engineer, or I'm an accountant.

1288
01:15:21,960 --> 01:15:31,510
Need that A or N. It's absolutely vital to stress the need for an article either A or N before your job title because your job position is almost always a countable noun.

1289
01:15:32,250 --> 01:15:35,480
Our guidance specifically warns against a common and jarring mistake.

1290
01:15:35,880 --> 01:15:38,020
Just make sure you're not saying I'm engineer.

1291
01:15:38,060 --> 01:15:38,780
That's wrong.

1292
01:15:38,840 --> 01:15:40,190
Yeah, that definitely sounds off.

1293
01:15:40,350 --> 01:15:43,470
This omission instantly flags a non-native speaker.

1294
01:15:43,850 --> 01:15:48,040
So getting the article right is a simple but powerful way to enhance your fluency.

1295
01:15:48,880 --> 01:15:56,470
If you hold a truly unique role within your company where you are the sole person in that specific position, you can use the definite article they.

1296
01:15:57,270 --> 01:16:01,560
I am the CEO of a tech company or I'm the owner of a florist.

1297
01:16:01,580 --> 01:16:03,120
The for the one and only.

1298
01:16:03,300 --> 01:16:04,080
Exactly.

1299
01:16:04,060 --> 01:16:10,850
You can also talk more generally about the industry you work in by using the preposition in a very common and natural construction.

1300
01:16:11,410 --> 01:16:18,080
I work in plus industry, such as I work in marketing, I work in construction, I work in design, or I work in finance.

1301
01:16:18,100 --> 01:16:19,200
Working for the industry.

1302
01:16:19,300 --> 01:16:19,600
Good one.

1303
01:16:20,000 --> 01:16:25,490
This gives a broader and helpful sense of your professional field, providing context for your specific role.

1304
01:16:26,290 --> 01:16:31,620
When you want to specify which company or organization employs you, you have two excellent prepositional options.

1305
01:16:32,010 --> 01:16:33,810
at or for with the company name.

1306
01:16:34,450 --> 01:16:37,870
You could say, I work at Vogue or I work at an architecture firm.

1307
01:16:38,230 --> 01:16:41,180
Alternatively, I work for the government or I work for Google.

1308
01:16:41,880 --> 01:16:55,790
Both prepositions are grammatically correct and are often used interchangeably in many contexts, though for can sometimes imply a slightly more direct employment relationship or sense of loyalty, while at can be more general about your workplace location.

1309
01:16:56,410 --> 01:16:58,500
Choose the one that feels most natural to you.

1310
01:16:59,040 --> 01:17:00,360
At or for.

1311
01:17:00,790 --> 01:17:01,490
both work.

1312
01:17:02,160 --> 01:17:07,210
This raises an important and frequently asked question for entrepreneurs, consultants and freelancers.

1313
01:17:08,190 --> 01:17:16,300
How do you articulate self-employment clearly, concisely and professionally when introducing yourself without sounding informal or vague?

1314
01:17:16,400 --> 01:17:16,740
Yeah.

1315
01:17:16,760 --> 01:17:17,930
What's the best way to say that?

1316
01:17:17,990 --> 01:17:22,850
Our research provides excellent and concise options for this specific and increasingly common scenario.

1317
01:17:23,380 --> 01:17:25,080
You can directly state, I work for myself.

1318
01:17:25,340 --> 01:17:25,620
Clear.

1319
01:17:25,980 --> 01:17:29,480
Another straightforward and highly professional option is, I'm self-employed.

1320
01:17:29,880 --> 01:17:31,700
This is a widely understood and accepted term.

1321
01:17:31,840 --> 01:17:32,820
Also very standard.

1322
01:17:33,240 --> 01:17:37,290
Or you could say, I run my own business, perhaps adding, specializing in your field.

1323
01:17:38,290 --> 01:17:47,570
All three convey the same message effectively, allowing you to choose the one that feels most natural and relevant for your specific conversation and context while maintaining a professional image.

1324
01:17:47,690 --> 01:17:48,450
Good options there.

1325
01:17:49,210 --> 01:17:59,490
Here's a fantastic, actionable hint that comes directly from our deep dive, incredibly valuable for anyone navigating professional introductions, especially in a specific industry.

1326
01:18:00,530 --> 01:18:10,490
If you're ever unsure about precisely how to talk about your job or your industry, the best practical advice is to listen to others who are from your industry introduce themselves.

1327
01:18:10,470 --> 01:18:12,800
Ah, learn from the locals, so to speak.

1328
01:18:12,810 --> 01:18:13,380
Exactly.

1329
01:18:13,420 --> 01:18:16,300
This is real world peer learning at its best.

1330
01:18:16,860 --> 01:18:24,880
Think about watching TED Talks, tuning into industry specific webinars or exploring lots of industry relevant videos on platforms like YouTube or LinkedIn.

1331
01:18:25,580 --> 01:18:31,790
These platforms are rich with examples of seasoned professionals articulating their roles, their companies and their contributions.

1332
01:18:32,510 --> 01:18:34,150
So much content out there.

1333
01:18:34,230 --> 01:18:47,490
Observing how others phrase their introductions, the vocabulary they use, and the tone they adopt can give you invaluable insights into common terminology, appropriate levels of formality, and effective structures specific to your sector.

1334
01:18:48,590 --> 01:18:57,000
This strategy can significantly enhance your business English communication skills, helping you sound more authentic and integrated into your professional community.

1335
01:18:57,520 --> 01:19:02,360
It's like getting a masterclass in professional self-presentation, tailor-made for your field.

1336
01:19:02,480 --> 01:19:03,740
Great practical advice.

1337
01:19:04,420 --> 01:19:06,510
Immerse yourself in the language of your industry.

1338
01:19:06,850 --> 01:19:08,970
Now, let's touch on education and qualifications.

1339
01:19:09,390 --> 01:19:20,970
What's truly fascinating here, especially from a strategic communication perspective, is how this information is most commonly and most appropriately integrated into an introduction within a highly professional context.

1340
01:19:21,090 --> 01:19:21,290
Right.

1341
01:19:21,310 --> 01:19:23,290
Not usually the first thing you mention at a party.

1342
01:19:23,270 --> 01:19:23,890
Exactly.

1343
01:19:24,510 --> 01:19:33,240
Think about scenarios like a job interview, a professional conference where you're a speaker, or when establishing credibility with new colleagues or potential clients.

1344
01:19:33,810 --> 01:19:38,490
It's about demonstrating your foundational knowledge, your expertise and your commitment to a field.

1345
01:19:39,110 --> 01:19:42,880
However, and this is crucial, it needs to be contextually appropriate.

1346
01:19:43,440 --> 01:19:51,990
As we discussed earlier, you wouldn't typically lead with your degree at a casual social gathering, but it's absolutely essential when building a robust professional persona.

1347
01:19:52,240 --> 01:19:53,470
Okay, so context again.

1348
01:19:53,930 --> 01:19:54,870
When it's relevant.

1349
01:19:54,890 --> 01:19:55,390
Precisely.

1350
01:19:56,070 --> 01:20:08,720
When discussing your degrees, a straightforward and universally understood approach is, I have a degree in plus industry subject, such as I have a degree in architecture or design, or I have a degree in engineering.

1351
01:20:08,700 --> 01:20:14,300
This clearly states your area of academic specialization, providing immediate insight into your expertise.

1352
01:20:14,320 --> 01:20:15,060
Simple and direct.

1353
01:20:15,280 --> 01:20:20,790
If you want to emphasize your professional status or the fact that you hold a formal certification, our research offers.

1354
01:20:21,190 --> 01:20:23,200
I am a qualified plus job title, for example.

1355
01:20:23,260 --> 01:20:25,460
I'm a qualified engineer or I'm a qualified architect.

1356
01:20:25,840 --> 01:20:26,760
Adding qualified.

1357
01:20:27,260 --> 01:20:28,240
What's the nuance there?

1358
01:20:28,340 --> 01:20:32,390
The key nuance here, which we must underscore, is clearly explained.

1359
01:20:33,150 --> 01:20:44,860
The adjective qualified specifically clarifies your official status or licensure, making it highly relevant in certain contexts, particularly an interview where your credentials are being assessed.

1360
01:20:45,770 --> 01:20:48,230
So it confirms you have the official paperwork, basically.

1361
01:20:48,250 --> 01:20:48,670
Exactly.

1362
01:20:49,110 --> 01:20:56,420
However, as we noted, it's generally unnecessary and perhaps even a little stiff at a casual party or a social event.

1363
01:20:56,620 --> 01:21:02,020
In that more relaxed context, you would typically just say, I'm an engineer or I'm an architect.

1364
01:21:02,920 --> 01:21:11,140
Understanding this distinction allows you to tailor your introduction for maximum impact and appropriateness without oversharing or sounding overly formal.

1365
01:21:11,320 --> 01:21:14,440
Use qualified when it really matters for credibility.

1366
01:21:14,600 --> 01:21:15,350
That's the idea.

1367
01:21:15,850 --> 01:21:21,130
For those currently pursuing higher education or continuous learning, several expressions are really useful and highly professional.

1368
01:21:21,690 --> 01:21:27,220
I'm studying plus industry degree name, such as I'm studying environmental science or I'm studying applied science.

1369
01:21:27,840 --> 01:21:30,100
This clearly communicates your current academic pursuit.

1370
01:21:30,090 --> 01:21:31,110
Okay, straightforward.

1371
01:21:31,450 --> 01:21:40,030
For advanced research, you might say, I'm completing a PhD in plus research area, like public health or artificial intelligence ethics.

1372
01:21:40,770 --> 01:21:43,620
This positions you at the forefront of academic inquiry.

1373
01:21:43,940 --> 01:21:45,440
Signals higher level study.

1374
01:21:45,780 --> 01:21:51,560
To indicate you're nearing the end of your studies, you can state, I'm in my final year of degree name, for example.

1375
01:21:51,590 --> 01:21:53,550
I'm in my final year of an economics degree.

1376
01:21:54,210 --> 01:21:56,910
Or, more generally, I'm nearing the end of my studies.

1377
01:21:57,710 --> 01:21:59,650
This offers context about your timeline.

1378
01:22:00,010 --> 01:22:01,850
Good for managing expectations, maybe.

1379
01:22:02,010 --> 01:22:10,150
And if your training is less formal, but still relevant to your professional development, our material provides excellent options that highlight initiative.

1380
01:22:10,990 --> 01:22:17,820
I'm currently taking an online course to upskill in data analytics, or I'm doing an extra course to improve my skills in project management.

1381
01:22:18,000 --> 01:22:19,680
Shows you're proactive about learning.

1382
01:22:19,850 --> 01:22:20,370
Exactly.

1383
01:22:20,990 --> 01:22:30,670
This shows initiative, a commitment to continuous learning and adaptability, all of which are highly valued attributes in any dynamic business environment.

1384
01:22:31,390 --> 01:22:42,790
The analysis here is that these options allow for remarkable precision and relevance, ensuring you present your academic background and ongoing professional development effectively and strategically for any given situation.

1385
01:22:42,770 --> 01:22:53,870
This strategic choice in an introduction can significantly impact how your professional capabilities, your intellectual curiosity, and your dedication are perceived, offering a strong boost to your professional brand.

1386
01:22:54,160 --> 01:22:56,200
